{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 and a key figure in the Crusader states during the twelfth century. He was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem and inherited the county at a young age after his father was assassinated. His early rule was marked by a regency led by his mother and later by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. Raymond was known for his political acumen and his ability to navigate the complex alliances and rivalries among the Crusader nobility. He was also a cousin to the kings of Jerusalem which gave him significant influence in court affairs.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a central role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was appointed regent of Jerusalem in 1174 after the death of King Amalric I and the minority of Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was chosen for his experience and his reputation for loyalty. His regency lasted until 1177 when he was removed from power after being accused of treason by the nobles who favored Guy of Lusignan. The accusations were likely politically motivated as Raymond had opposed Guy's rise and had aligned himself with the military orders who distrusted Guy. Raymond was briefly imprisoned but later released and restored to his title as Count of Tripoli.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was also a military leader who fought in several key battles against Saladin. He participated in the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army suffered a devastating defeat. He was captured during the battle and spent time in captivity before being released. After his release he returned to Tripoli and worked to rebuild the defenses of his county. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem and before the arrival of the Third Crusade. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli as his successors struggled to maintain its independence. Raymond III is remembered as a skilled diplomat and a cautious leader who tried to preserve the fragile Crusader states amid growing Muslim pressure. His legacy is tied to his efforts to balance internal politics and external threats during a turbulent period in the Levant.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.9740936160087585}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -7.271740287251305e-06}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.2927336096763611}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.5882569551467896}, {"token": " key", "logprob": -0.7598369121551514}, {"token": " figure", "logprob": -0.7225791215896606}, {"to